dition | Snapshot | Final | Evidence or derivation path |\\n|---|---|---|---|---|\\n| 1 | Root cause of the CLAUDE_CODE_SESSION_ID split is identified | AVAILABLE | AVAILABLE | Fact-Check Claim 1 VERIFIED: MCP server gets `cse_...` format; Bash subprocesses get UUID format; verified via `/proc/PID/environ` observation |\\n| 2 | File and function to change identified | AVAILABLE | AVAILABLE | Fact-Check Claim 4 VERIFIED: `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/server.py` line 1140 defines `_read_gate_token()` |\\n| 3 | Proposed fix code (Option A) is provided in the item | AVAILABLE | AVAILABLE | Fact-Check Claim 5 VERIFIED: Option A pseudocode with exact path + fallback loop provided in item body |\\n| 4 | Existing test coverage for _read_gate_token() | DERIVABLE | DERIVABLE | Self-resolved via Bash: 9 grep hits found in test suite. Test `test_gate_token_file_readable_at_runtime()` at line 176 is marked `@pytest.mark.e2e` and covers primary path only (exact session ID). No unit test covers the new directory-scan fallback path \u2014 **gap identified but gap is derivable**: write new unit test that simulates session ID mismatch and verifies fallback finds token in alternative subdirectory |\\n| 5 | Whether get-gate-token.mjs needs changes under Option A | DERIVABLE | AVAILABLE | Self-resolved via Read: `get-gate-token.mjs` lines 7-22 write to `{dhRoot}/sessions/{sessionId}/.gate-token` using `process.env.CLAUDE_CODE_SESSION_ID`. Option A scans all subdirectories in `sessions/` for any `.gate-token` file. Script needs **zero changes** \u2014 it continues writing to its current path; fallback finds it regardless. Derivation confirmed by code review. |\\n\\n**Counts**: AVAILABLE 4, DERIVABLE 1, MISSING 0\\n\\n**Verdict**: APPROVED\\n\\n**Changes from snapshot**:\\n- Condition 4: DERIVABLE \u2192 DERIVABLE (unchanged; gap is acceptable for planning because unit test is derivative work that can be generated during implementation)\\n- Condition 5: DERIVABLE \u2192 AVAILABLE (resolved: get-gate-token.mjs requires no changes under Option A)\\n\\n**Additional Notes**:\\n- Impact Radius section correctly identifies the test coverage gap as MEDIUM risk under \\\"Risk Summary\\\": \\\"the new fallback code path (iterating `~/.dh/sessions/` subdirectories) has no unit test coverage\\\"\\n- The gap does not block planning or acceptance criteria writing. The planner can assign test authorship as part of the implementation task.\\n- All 6 claims from Fact-Check verified successfully \u2014 no REFUTED claims present.\\n- Condition count increased to 5 from original 5-item snapshot because Fact-Check findings and Impact Radius expanded the analysis scope.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Impact\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n`backlog_add` (and any gate-protected MCP tool) is completely broken in remote execution environments. The workaround is bypassing the MCP tool and creating GitHub issues directly via `mcp__github__issue_write`.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Impact Radius\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n### Code \u2014 Producers (write the changed interface)\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/server.py::_read_gate_token` \u2014 The function being modified. Currently constructs a single path `~/.dh/sessions/{CLAUDE_CODE_SESSION_ID}/.gate-token` and returns None on OSError. The fix adds a fallback loop over all subdirectories under `~/.dh/sessions/` when the primary path is not found. The function signature and return type (`str | None`) are unchanged. | Risk: LOW | Why: isolated internal function, single call site within server.py, no external callers outside the test suite\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/skills/work-backlog-item/scripts/get-gate-token.mjs` \u2014 Token producer: generates a 32-byte hex token, creates `~/.dh/sessions/{CLAUDE_CODE_SESSION_ID}/.gate-token`, and prints the token to stdout. Under Option A the script is unchanged. If Option B is pursued instead, this script must also write to a fixed path (e.g. `~/.dh/sessions/.current-gate-token`). | Risk: LOW (Option A) / MEDIUM (Option B/C) | Why: Option A requires no change; Options B/C require coordinated writes here and reads in server.py\\n\\n### Code \u2014 Consumers (read the changed interface)\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/server.py::backlog_add` \u2014 The only caller of `_read_gate_token()`. Reads the returned token and compares it against the caller-supplied `gate_token` parameter. Post-fix behaviour: the comparison logic is unchanged; `_read_gate_token()` now returns a valid token in remote sessions where it previously returned None. No migration needed. | Risk: LOW | Why: calling code is unaffected \u2014 if the function now returns a token instead of None, backlog_add proceeds normally\\n\\n### Code \u2014 Other References\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/tests/conftest.py::_patch_gate_token` \u2014 Autouse fixture that writes a fixed token to `{tmp_path}/dh_state/sessions/{_TEST_SESSION_ID}/.gate-token` and sets `CLAUDE_CODE_SESSION_ID` and `DH_STATE_HOME`. The fixture sets a known exact session ID, so the primary path in `_read_gate_token()` resolves correctly for all non-e2e tests. The fallback loop is never exercised. Fixture remains correct as-is. | Risk: LOW | Why: fixture controls the env vars so the primary path always hits; no behaviour change for unit tests\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/tests/conftest.py::gate_token` \u2014 Returns the fixed `TEST_GATE_TOKEN` string. No change needed. | Risk: LOW | Why: string constant, unaffected by path lookup changes\\n\\n### Tests\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/tests/test_backlog_core_server.py::test_gate_token_file_readable_at_runtime` \u2014 e2e test (`@pytest.mark.e2e`). Calls `_read_gate_token()` and asserts `token is not None` and `len(token) == 64`. In a remote execution environment this test would have failed before the fix (same split-session-ID bug). After the fix it should pass via the fallback path. However, the test still asserts the exact 64-char token length \u2014 the fallback path returns whatever token was written, which is always 64 hex chars, so the assertion holds. **Gap**: no unit test covers the fallback directory-scan path (i.e. the scenario where the exact session ID subdirectory does not exist but another subdirectory does). A new unit test is needed. | Risk: MEDIUM | Why: the new fallback code path has zero unit test coverage\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/tests/test_scenarios.py` \u2014 Uses `TEST_GATE_TOKEN` from conftest. All calls to `backlog_add` pass `gate_token=_SESSION_GATE_TOKEN`. The autouse fixture ensures the primary path resolves. No behaviour change. | Risk: LOW | Why: controlled test environment, unaffected by fallback logic\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/tests/test_live_validation.py` \u2014 Imports `_read_gate_token` directly and calls it to supply the `gate_token` parameter to `backlog_add`. Sets `CLAUDE_CODE_SESSION_ID` and `DH_STATE_HOME` to a unique per-run session ID (exact path). After the fix the primary path still resolves for live tests. No behaviour change. | Risk: LOW | Why: live tests construct an exact session path; fallback is never triggered\\n\\n### Documentation\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/skills/work-backlog-item/SKILL.md` \u2014 Contains the `<gate_token>` frontmatter block: `!node \\\"${CLAUDE_SKILL_DIR}/scripts/get-gate-token.mjs\\\"`. Documents that the script writes the token at skill load time. No content is wrong after Option A \u2014 the fix is transparent to skill users. Under Option B/C, the documented script behaviour would change and this file would need an update note. | Risk: LOW (Option A) | Why: Option A is transparent; the skill's description of how the token is generated and injected remains accurate\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/skills/work-backlog-item/references/workflows/create/start.md` \u2014 Instructs the agent to call `backlog_add(gate_token=\\\"<gate_token>\\\")`. The fix does not change how the gate_token parameter is used. No update needed. | Risk: LOW | Why: invocation pattern unchanged\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/skills/work-backlog-item/references/workflows/quick/start.md` \u2014 Same pattern as create/start.md. | Risk: LOW | Why: invocation pattern unchanged\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/ARCHITECTURE.md` \u2014 No references to gate token or session ID found. No update needed. | Risk: LOW | Why: no relevant claims\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/docs/workflow-architecture-diagram.md` \u2014 References `active-task-{CLAUDE_CODE_SESSION_ID}.json` context files. No gate token documentation found. No update needed. | Risk: LOW | Why: unrelated to gate token mechanism\\n\\n### Configuration / CI\\n\\n- `.github/workflows/code-quality.yml` (and other CI workflows) \u2014 No references to `CLAUDE_CODE_SESSION_ID`, `gate_token`, or `get-gate-token.mjs` found in any workflow file. The e2e tests (`@pytest.mark.e2e`) are not run in CI (they require a live Claude session). Unit and scenario tests use the `_patch_gate_token` autouse fixture which is CI-safe. | Risk: LOW | Why: CI does not exercise the gate token path; autouse fixture covers unit tests\\n\\n### Agent Instructions\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/agents/impact-analyst.md` \u2014 References `gate_token` and `backlog_add` in system instructions. The instruction text tells the agent to call `backlog_add` with the injected gate token. Fix is transparent \u2014 the token injection mechanism works the same way post-fix. | Risk: LOW | Why: the agent instruction describes the call pattern, not the server-side resolution logic\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/agents/backlog-item-groomer.md` \u2014 References `backlog_add`. No change to the parameter contract. | Risk: LOW | Why: call pattern unchanged\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/skills/start-task/SKILL.md` \u2014 References `CLAUDE_CODE_SESSION_ID` for `sam_active_task` context, not for gate token. Unrelated to the fix. | Risk: LOW | Why: different use of the same env var\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/skills/implementation-manager/SKILL.md` \u2014 Uses `CLAUDE_CODE_SESSION_ID` to locate the active-task context file. Unrelated to gate token path resolution. | Risk: LOW | Why: different subsystem\\n\\n### Systems Inventory\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/server.py::_read_gate_token` | Role: producer | Connection: the function being changed; adds directory-scan fallback | Action: CODE_CHANGE | Risk: LOW\\n- `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/server.py::backlog_add` | Role: consumer | Connection: sole caller of `_read_gate_token()`; comparison logic unchanged | Action: VERIFY_COMPATIBLE | Risk: LOW\\n- `plugins/development-harness/skills/work-backlog-item/scripts/get-gate-token.mjs` | Role: producer | Connection: writes the .gate-token file that `_read_gate_token()` reads; unchanged under Option A | Action: VERIFY_COMPATIBLE | Risk: LOW\\n- `plugins/development-harness/tests/conftest.py::_patch_gate_token` | Role: test | Connection: autouse fixture that sets up the exact-path scenario for all unit tests | Action: VERIFY_COMPATIBLE | Risk: LOW\\n- `plugins/development-harness/tests/test_backlog_core_server.py::test_gate_token_file_readable_at_runtime` | Role: test | Connection: e2e test calling `_read_gate_token()` directly; does not cover the new fallback path | Action: TEST_UPDATE | Risk: MEDIUM\\n- `plugins/development-harness/tests/test_scenarios.py` | Role: test | Connection: calls `backlog_add` with `gate_token`; covered by autouse fixture | Action: VERIFY_COMPATIBLE | Risk: LOW\\n- `plugins/development-harness/tests/test_live_validation.py` | Role: test | Connection: imports and calls `_read_gate_token()` directly for live validation | Action: VERIFY_COMPATIBLE | Risk: LOW\\n- `plugins/development-harness/skills/work-backlog-item/SKILL.md` | Role: agent-instruction | Connection: documents gate token injection via `<gate_token>` block | Action: VERIFY_COMPATIBLE | Risk: LOW\\n- `plugins/development-harness/skills/work-backlog-item/references/workflows/create/start.md` | Role: agent-instruction | Connection: instructs agent to call `backlog_add(gate_token=...)` | Action: VERIFY_COMPATIBLE | Risk: LOW\\n- `plugins/development-harness/skills/work-backlog-item/references/workflows/quick/start.md` | Role: agent-instruction | Connection: same gate_token call pattern | Action: VERIFY_COMPATIBLE | Risk: LOW\\n- `plugins/development-harness/agents/impact-analyst.md` | Role: agent-instruction | Connection: references `backlog_add` with gate_token | Action: VERIFY_COMPATIBLE | Risk: LOW\\n\\n### Risk Summary\\n\\n- Overall system risk: LOW\\n- Highest-risk systems:\\n  - `plugins/development-harness/tests/test_backlog_core_server.py` \u2014 the new fallback scan path (iterating `~/.dh/sessions/` subdirectories) has no unit test coverage; a regression could go undetected\\n  - `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/server.py::_read_gate_token` \u2014 the fallback scan introduces a subtle security surface: if multiple `.gate-token` files exist across session subdirectories (e.g. stale tokens from prior sessions), the first match wins \u2014 no ordering guarantee. This is an operational risk worth noting even though it is low probability\\n- Main risk themes:\\n  - verification: new fallback code path has no dedicated unit test\\n  - operational: non-deterministic token selection when multiple valid token files exist across session directories\\n- Scope expansion:\\n  - None \u2014 the impact surface is exactly as described in the item. All affected files were within the known `backlog_core/server.py` and `tests/` perimeter.\\n\\n### Ecosystem Completeness Checklist\\n\\n- [ ] Every code producer updated or verified compatible\\n- [ ] Every code consumer migrated to new interface\\n- [ ] Every stale document updated\\n- [ ] Every agent instruction updated\\n- [ ] Old interface deprecated or removed (if replacing)\\n- [ ] CI/config files updated and validated\\n- [ ] New unit test added for the fallback directory-scan path (missing coverage gap)\\n\\n<!-- artifact-gist:fc3f023a4cb751f1ad1c350c015c808a -->\\n</div>\\n\\n## Groomed (2026-05-22)\\n\\n### Reproducibility\\n\\n1. Open a remote claude.ai/code session (not a local terminal session).\\n2. Invoke any skill that calls `backlog_add` (e.g., `/dh:create-backlog-item` or `/dh:work-backlog-item create -- \\\"test item\\\"`).\\n3. Observe the error returned: \\\"Gate token file could not be read\\\" or similar OSError.\\n4. To confirm the split: in the same session, run `echo $CLAUDE_CODE_SESSION_ID` in a Bash subprocess and compare the value to the session ID visible in the MCP server environment \u2014 one will be a UUID (`81fe5398-...`), the other will be a `cse_...` prefixed API session ID.\\n\\n### Priority\\n\\n8/10 \u2014 `backlog_add` is completely broken in remote execution environments (claude.ai/code sessions). Any skill or agent that creates backlog items fails silently or with an opaque error for all remote users. The gate token mechanism exists to prevent accidental creation of duplicate or malformed items, but the session ID split makes the gate permanently closed in these environments regardless of intent.\\n\\n### Impact\\n\\n- Blocks: all `backlog_add` calls in remote claude.ai/code sessions \u2014 100% failure rate in that environment\\n- Blocks: any skill or agent workflow that creates backlog items remotely (e.g., `/dh:create-backlog-item`, `/dh:work-backlog-item`)\\n- Bottleneck: remote users cannot use the backlog creation workflow at all; they must switch to a local session or manually create items via `gh issue create`\\n- No impact on local sessions where both MCP server and Bash subprocesses see the same UUID-format session ID\\n\\n### Benefits\\n\\n- Restores `backlog_add` functionality in remote execution environments (claude.ai/code sessions)\\n- Unblocks all skills and agents that depend on backlog creation in remote sessions\\n- Eliminates a class of environment-specific failures that are opaque and hard to diagnose for users\\n- Makes the gate token mechanism environment-agnostic \u2014 it works regardless of which session ID format the runtime provides\\n\\n### Expected Behavior\\n\\nWhen a skill calls `backlog_add` in a remote claude.ai/code session, the gate token written by `get-gate-token.mjs` is successfully located and read by the MCP server's `_read_gate_token()` function, regardless of whether the session ID used when writing the token differs in format from the session ID visible to the MCP server process. The `backlog_add` call completes successfully and the new item appears in the backlog.\\n\\n### Acceptance Criteria\\n\\n1. In a remote claude.ai/code session, invoking a skill that calls `backlog_add` succeeds and the new item appears in `backlog_list` output \u2014 no \\\"Gate token file could not be read\\\" error is returned.\\n2. In a local session (where session IDs match), `backlog_add` continues to succeed as before \u2014 no regression.\\n3. When `CLAUDE_CODE_SESSION_ID` in the MCP server environment is a `cse_...` prefixed value and the token file was written under a UUID-format session directory, `_read_gate_token()` returns a non-None value.\\n4. When no gate token file exists anywhere under `~/.dh/sessions/`, `_read_gate_token()` returns None and `backlog_add` returns its normal \\\"no gate token\\\" error (not an unhandled OSError).\\n5. `uv run pytest` passes (no new test failures introduced).\\n\\n### Resources\\n\\n| Type | Item |\\n|------|------|\\n| File | `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/server.py` (function `_read_gate_token`, line ~1140) |\\n| File | `plugins/development-harness/scripts/get-gate-token.mjs` (gate token writer \u2014 no changes needed) |\\n\\n### Files\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/server.py` \u2014 `_read_gate_token()` at line ~1140: the function that fails to locate the token file when session IDs differ in format\\n- `plugins/development-harness/scripts/get-gate-token.mjs` \u2014 writes the gate token file using the session ID from the Bash subprocess environment; confirmed no changes needed here\\n\\n### Dependencies\\n\\n- Depends on: None\\n- Blocks: None directly \u2014 this is a self-contained defect fix. Skills that use `backlog_add` in remote sessions are unblocked as a side effect of this fix.\\n\\n### Effort\\n\\nSmall \u2014 the defect is isolated to a single function in one file (`_read_gate_token` in `server.py`). Root cause is fully verified. The change scope is narrow: add a fallback path scan when the primary session-ID-based path yields no file. No schema changes, no new dependencies, no cross-plugin impact. Medium accuracy risk due to absence of existing unit tests for this code path \u2014 a new test covering the fallback scenario is needed alongside the fix.\\n\\n### Design Intent Alignment\\n\\n**Alignment assessment**: NOT_APPLICABLE\\n\\n**Rationale**: This is a defect fix for a runtime environment incompatibility. The fix (adding a fallback scan in `_read_gate_token()`) restores the intended behavior \u2014 gate token validation works \u2014 without changing any public interface, API contract, or design intent. No design intent alignment review is needed for a behavioral regression fix.\\n\\n### Issue Classification\\n\\n**Type**: defect\\n\\n**Rationale**: The item describes a traceable failure with an identifiable cause chain. The MCP server process and Bash subprocesses receive different values for `CLAUDE_CODE_SESSION_ID` (API session ID vs UUID format). This causes the gate token file to be written by `get-gate-token.mjs` to one directory path and read by `_read_gate_token()` from a different directory path, resulting in `OSError` \u2192 `None` \u2192 gate token validation failure on every `backlog_add` call in remote sessions.\\n\\n**Analysis Method**: 5-whys root-cause analysis via /find-cause skill\\n\\n**Scenario Target**: Gate token validation fails in remote execution environments (backlog_add blocked) \u2192 Gate token is reliably found and validated in both local and remote execution environments\\n\\n### Root-Cause Analysis\\n\\n**Method**: 5-whys root-cause analysis\\n\\n**Evidence chain**:\\n1. Observed failure: `backlog_add` calls fail with \\\"Gate token file could not be read\\\" in remote execution environments\\n2. Why: `_read_gate_token()` returns `None` due to `OSError` when reading `~/.dh/sessions/{mcp_session_id}/.gate-token`\\n3. Why: The file does not exist at that path; the gate token was written to a different path by `get-gate-token.mjs`\\n4. Why: `get-gate-token.mjs` constructs its path using `process.env.CLAUDE_CODE_SESSION_ID` (UUID format) while `_read_gate_token()` uses `_os.environ.get(\\\"CLAUDE_CODE_SESSION_ID\\\")` (API session ID format)\\n5. Why: In remote execution environments, the MCP server process inherits the API session ID (`cse_...` format) at startup, while Bash subprocesses spawned later receive a UUID value; the runtime assigns different values depending on process launch context\\n\\n**Root cause**: The runtime assigns `CLAUDE_CODE_SESSION_ID` as two distinct values in remote execution environments: the MCP server process receives the API session ID (`cse_...` format), while Bash subprocesses receive a UUID (`81fe5398-...` format). Both `_read_gate_token()` and `get-gate-token.mjs` correctly use the value they receive, but this value mismatch causes them to operate on different directory paths.\\n\\n**Fix locus**: \\n- Primary: `backlog_core/server.py:_read_gate_token()` \u2014 implement fallback directory scanning when primary path does not exist (Option A in item)\\n- Secondary: `skills/work-backlog-item/scripts/get-gate-token.mjs` \u2014 conditionally write to multiple paths if needed under Options B or C\\n\\n## Problem\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nIn remote execution environments (claude.ai/code sessions), the MCP server process and Bash subprocesses see **different values** for `CLAUDE_CODE_SESSION_ID`:\\n\\n| Context | `CLAUDE_CODE_SESSION_ID` |\\n|---|---|\\n| MCP server process (launched at session init) | `cse_014UsehEvkAnG9sAHjk7bdkV` |\\n| Bash subprocesses | `81fe5398-c20e-456b-87cc-f09c2c68df74` |\\n\\n`get-gate-token.mjs` runs as a Bash subprocess and writes the token to:\\n```\\n~/.dh/sessions/81fe5398-c20e-456b-87cc-f09c2c68df74/.gate-token\\n```\\n\\nThe MCP server's `_read_gate_token()` reads from:\\n```\\n~/.dh/sessions/cse_014UsehEvkAnG9sAHjk7bdkV/.gate-token\\n```\\n\\nThe file is never at the path the server reads \u2192 `OSError \u2192 None \u2192 \\\"Gate token file could not be read\\\"` on every `backlog_add` call in remote sessions.\\n\\n**Verified by**: reading `/proc/638/environ` (MCP server PID) vs `echo $CLAUDE_CODE_SESSION_ID` in Bash.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Root Cause\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n`CLAUDE_CODE_SESSION_ID` is assigned two different values depending on when and how a process was launched:\\n- The MCP server inherits the **API session ID** (`cse_...` format) at session startup\\n- Bash subprocesses spawned later see a **UUID** (`81fe5398-...` format)\\n\\nThe gate token mechanism assumes both sides use the same value \u2014 this assumption breaks in remote execution.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Fix Options\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n**Option A (minimal \u2014 recommended):** In `_read_gate_token()` in `backlog_core/server.py`, fall back to scanning all subdirectories of `~/.dh/sessions/` for a `.gate-token` file when the primary path is not found. Low risk \u2014 this function is only used for gate validation.\\n\\n```python\\ndef _read_gate_token() -> str | None:\\n    session_id = _os.environ.get(\\\"CLAUDE_CODE_SESSION_ID\\\")\\n    if not session_id:\\n        return None\\n    dh_root = Path(_os.environ.get(\\\"DH_STATE_HOME\\\", \\\"\\\")).expanduser() if _os.environ.get(\\\"DH_STATE_HOME\\\") else Path.home() / \\\".dh\\\"\\n    sessions_dir = dh_root / \\\"sessions\\\"\\n    # Try exact session ID first\\n    try:\\n        return (sessions_dir / session_id / \\\".gate-token\\\").read_text(encoding=\\\"utf-8\\\").strip()\\n    except OSError:\\n        pass\\n    # Remote execution fallback: MCP server gets cse_* ID, bash gets UUID\\n    try:\\n        for candidate in sessions_dir.iterdir():\\n            p = candidate / \\\".gate-token\\\"\\n            if p.exists():\\n                return p.read_text(encoding=\\\"utf-8\\\").strip()\\n    except OSError:\\n        pass\\n    return None\\n```\\n\\n**Option B:** In `get-gate-token.mjs`, also write to a fixed-name file (`~/.dh/sessions/.current-gate-token`) that the server reads first, bypassing session ID entirely.\\n\\n**Option C:** Expose the MCP server's `CLAUDE_CODE_SESSION_ID` via an env file or MCP tool so `get-gate-token.mjs` can write to the correct path.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Files To Change\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/server.py` \u2014 `_read_gate_token()` function\\n- `plugins/development-harness/skills/work-backlog-item/scripts/get-gate-token.mjs` (if Option B or C)\\n</div>\\n\\n## Summary\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nAll six claims in issue #2406 are VERIFIED against source code and issue documentation:\\n1. \u2713 Session ID split exists (MCP vs Bash subprocess)\\n2. \u2713 get-gate-token.mjs writes using bash session ID\\n3. \u2713 _read_gate_token() reads using MCP session ID  \\n4. \u2713 Function location correct\\n5. \u2713 Option A requires only server.py changes\\n6. \u2713 Gate-protected tools broken in remote execution\\n\\nRoot cause is confirmed: CLAUDE_CODE_SESSION_ID environment variable has different values at MCP startup vs Bash subprocess spawning time, creating path mismatch in gate token lookup.\\n</div>\\n\",\"digest\":\"532fde607677c502b6dd3b88dcc6a174b22003252b4be16127